  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "The Generalized Exponential Signal $e^{st}$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $e^{st}$ as the generalized continuous-time exponential form that unifies real exponentials, complex exponentials, and the damped or growing sinusoidal signals developed next.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Generalized Exponential Signal $e^{st}$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to The Generalized Exponential Signal $e^{st}$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read $x(t)=e^{st}$ as a continuous-time signal in the variable $t$, with $s$ held fixed as the parameter that sets the signal’s behavior.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{st}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When $s$ is fixed, the formula no longer names a general algebraic expression. It names one specific signal in time, and changing the value of $s$ changes which signal you get.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the simple real choice $s=-1$. Then the same form becomes the familiar decaying exponential $x(t)=e^{-t}$, which is the cleanest base case for this module.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is why $e^{st}$ is called the generalized exponential signal: the same template will later cover real exponentials first, then complex choices of $s$, and then the damped or growing sinusoidal cases that follow.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For now, keep the roles separate: $t$ is the variable, $s$ is the fixed parameter, and $e^{st}$ is the signal form that ties the later special cases together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Generalized Exponential Signal $e^{st}$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Reading $e^{st}$ as a Continuous-Time Signal with Fixed $s$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $s=-2+j3$. Write the corresponding signal as $x(t)=e^{st}$ and explain what stays fixed and what changes with time.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a fixed complex value of $s$. Once $s$ is fixed, $x(t)=e^{st}$ names one specific continuous-time signal in $t$, not a formula with two variables competing for the same role.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(-2+j3)t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the fixed parameter directly into the exponent: $s=-2+j3$ becomes $x(t)=e^{(-2+j3)t}$. The parameter stays fixed, and $t$ is the variable that traces the signal over time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the notation $x(t)=e^{st}$ means a concrete time signal once $s$ is chosen. This is the same generalized exponential form from the previous step, now read with one fixed parameter value instead of left abstract.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Generalized Exponential Signal $e^{st}$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Matching Real Values of $s$ to Real Exponential Signals",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $s=-3$. Match this value to the corresponding signal and classify it as growing, decaying, or constant.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is a real value of $s$. Once $s$ is fixed and real, $x(t)=e^{st}$ is a pure real exponential signal in $t$, and the sign of $s$ determines whether it grows, decays, or stays constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $s=-3$ into $x(t)=e^{st}$ to get $x(t)=e^{-3t}$. The parameter is fixed, and $t$ is the variable that traces the signal over time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The negative coefficient means the signal decays as $t$ increases, so the matching classification is decaying. If $s=0$, the same form would become $x(t)=e^{0t}=1$, which is the constant case.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Generalized Exponential Signal $e^{st}$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Matching Pure Imaginary Values of $s$ to Complex Exponential Signals",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $s=j4$. Match this value to the corresponding signal and interpret the result as a pure complex exponential.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is a pure imaginary value of $s$. When $s$ has no real part, $x(t)=e^{st}$ becomes a complex exponential with constant magnitude and oscillatory phase rather than growth or decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $s=j4$ into $x(t)=e^{st}$ to get $x(t)=e^{j4t}$. The parameter stays fixed, and $t$ is the variable that traces the oscillation over time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $s=j4$ matches the pure complex exponential $x(t)=e^{j4t}$. In Euler-style language, this is a constant-magnitude oscillation with no exponential growth or decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Generalized Exponential Signal $e^{st}$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Complex Values of $s$ as Combined Exponential and Oscillatory Behavior",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $s=-2+j5$. Separate $x(t)=e^{st}$ into envelope and oscillation factors and describe the resulting behavior.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a complex value of $s$ with both a real part and an imaginary part. Write $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$ so the real part sets the envelope rate and the imaginary part sets the oscillation rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(-2+j5)t}=e^{-2t}e^{j5t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $s=-2+j5$ into $x(t)=e^{st}$ and separate the result into the real-part factor and the imaginary-part factor. In general, $e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}=e^{\\sigma t}e^{j\\omega t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $e^{-2t}$ is the envelope and $e^{j5t}$ is the oscillation. Because the real part is negative, the envelope decays, so $s=-2+j5$ gives a damped complex exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
